# What each image type is, what an image dictionary must carry, and where
# this interpreter and the specification differ.
#
# PLRM 4.10.5: "Every image dictionary contains an entry named ImageType
# ... The most straightforward are type 1 dictionaries (LanguageLevel 2)
# ... Two more image types, 3 and 4 (LanguageLevel 3), represent masked
# images." Three types, and no PLRM table defines any other value.
#
# The family's interest is not the count but the reading. An image's
# samples are a bit stream, and so are a sampled function's samples and a
# mesh shading's vertices. Those two are read by one reader with an
# allowed-width table that refuses anything outside it. The image path had
# a third reader, written as a chain of depth cases with a bare final
# branch that assumed twelve bits, and nothing anywhere checked the width
# -- so three widths were accepted and read as though they were twelve.
#
# Membership is DERIVED: the guard offers every type code from -3 to 20
# and calls a type accepted when an image of it PAINTS.
#
#   <type>  <accepted|refused>  <reason>

1  accepted  samples painted straight, the only type imagemask also takes, PLRM Table 4.20
3  accepted  an image and an explicit mask, interleaved or separate, PLRM Table 4.22
4  accepted  an image whose masked pixels are named by a colour range, PLRM Table 4.25

types 3

# The widths a sample may be.
#
# PLRM Table 4.20: "Allowed values are 1, 2, 4, 8, and 12." That is a
# SHORTER list than a sampled function's, which PLRM 3.10.1 extends to
# 16, 24 and 32 -- so the two readers cannot share one table even though
# they share the problem, and the difference is the specification's rather
# than this interpreter's.
#
# PROBED, one image per width, with the samples packed the way that width
# requires: data of the wrong shape runs out, and running out raises what
# being refused raises, so a probe reusing one string would report every
# width as unsupported.
#
#   width <bits>  <takes|refuses>

width 1   takes
width 2   takes
width 4   takes
width 8   takes
width 12  takes
width 16  refuses
width 24  refuses
width 32  refuses
width 3   refuses
width 0   refuses

widths 10

# The entries a type 1 image dictionary must carry, PROBED by leaving each
# one out.
#
#   entry <name>  <required|optional>

entry ImageType         required
entry Width             required
entry Height            required
entry BitsPerComponent  required
entry ImageMatrix       required
entry Decode            required
entry DataSource        required
entry Interpolate       optional
entry MultipleDataSources optional

entries 9

# A required entry must come from the image dictionary and from nowhere
# else.
#
# The dictionary is made current so its entries can be read, which puts
# it on the dictionary stack above whatever the program already had there.
# An entry read as a bare name is then satisfied by any enclosing
# dictionary that happens to define that name, so an image missing a
# required entry painted from a value that was never part of it. A
# program with a typo got a plausible page instead of an error, and the
# names involved -- Width, Height, Decode -- are ordinary enough for a
# program to have defined for its own purposes.
#
# PROBED by defining each required entry in an enclosing dictionary and
# leaving it out of the image, for both operators that make an image
# dictionary current.
#
#   isolated <operator> <entry>  <isolated|leaks>

isolated image     Width             isolated
isolated image     Height            isolated
isolated image     BitsPerComponent  isolated
isolated image     ImageMatrix       isolated
isolated image     Decode            isolated
isolated imagemask ImageType         isolated
isolated imagemask Width             isolated
isolated imagemask Height            isolated
isolated imagemask Decode            isolated
isolated imagemask ImageMatrix       isolated

isolateds 10

# Where this interpreter and the specification differ, and what is to
# become of each difference.
#
#   <what>  <settled|thorn|heading>  <reason>

missing-required  settled  An entry the specification marks required and the dictionary does not carry raises undefined, because the entry is fetched and the fetch is what fails. The same is true of a halftone, a shading, a form, a function and a colour space, so it is one behaviour across six families rather than this one being odd

stencil-decode-not-held-to-its-two-values  settled  PLRM Table 4.20 says a stencil's Decode must be either [0 1] or [1 0]. A Decode of the right LENGTH carrying other numbers is accepted, and only its first element is consulted -- so [0.5 1] paints as [1 0] would. Accepting it is the common reading, and refusing it would make this interpreter the only one to enforce a rule PLRM states without making it an error. What IS held is the length: a Decode of another length describes something the stencil path cannot express, and taking its first element alone would let it stand for the whole

stencil-strictness-is-the-specification-s  settled  A stencil dictionary saying it has more than one data source is refused here, where it is commonly accepted, which is this interpreter being the stricter. PLRM Table 4.20 says the entry must be false or absent, so the strictness is the specification's and it stays. It is recorded because a refusal others do not make is worth knowing about even when the specification is behind it

divergences 3
